{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795269614,"nonce":"q2W7HomE/IX4yQgaYdgZ3AOEEZ4=","message":"Same defect seen again on the 0.1.21 release by hxn (peer session hxn-5f, 2026-11-21). Its physics checks in Chimpmonk and hxnme assert \"a warm step allocates nothing\" with hl.Gc.stats().totalAllocated. Stock HashLink 1.15 found real per-step allocations (FixedThreadPool queue nodes, one closure per job in the island solver). On Ash the same checks read 0, so they pass without testing anything. hxn tracks its side in its git-bug 2b08a2d5bb969751c5a6ce3161d684e05d67430776602de8ff40345c616014d5.\n\nReproduced here with the v0.1.21 macOS aarch64 release binary. Program: read totalAllocated, run `keep = [1, 2, 3]` (a static Dynamic field), read it again, 12 windows, then 10000 allocations in one window. Output in the form first window, later windows:\n\n- Default (TLAB on), hybrid, jit and interp alike: empty window 80 then 0; single allocation 928 then 0; 10000 allocations 1703936.\n- ASH_GC_TLAB=0, all three modes: empty window 160 then 80; single allocation 1168 then 240; 10000 allocations 1600080.\n\nWhat this shows:\n- With TLAB on, the counter moves only in whole 32768-byte regions. 1703936 is exactly 52 regions. A window with one allocation reads 0 unless a refill happens inside it. The first window after startup includes the first region (928), which is why a one-shot program can print a non-zero number and still be wrong.\n- With TLAB off the counter is exact: (240 - 80) = 160 bytes per [1, 2, 3], and (1600080 - 80) / 10000 = 160. The 80 is the cost of reading the counter.\n- The three engines agree, so the cause is the allocator accounting and not a tier.\n- hxn measured 144 bytes for the same allocation on stock HashLink 1.15 (Linux x86_64). Ash charges 160 with TLAB off. The 16 bytes may be size rounding or a header difference. Not investigated, and it does not affect the zero-versus-non-zero problem.\n- allocationCount is a constant 0 (documented at hlp_gc_stats in std/src/gc.rs), so a caller cannot use the object count either.\n\nWorkaround for a caller today: ASH_GC_TLAB=0 for the measuring run. The cost of that setting is in comment #1 above.\n\nNo Ash source changed.","files":null}]}